three of hong kong's most well-known  pro-democracy activists have been  sentenced to prison  joshua wong will spend around 13 months  in jail while agnes chao  will spend 10 months for their role in  last month's  last year's protests another activist  ivan lamb will be in prison for seven  months  demonstrations outside hong kong's  police headquarters in 2019 were  triggered by the introduction  of a controversial extradition bill  which has  since been shelved let's go live out of  hong kong al jazeera's adrian brown is  outside  the courthouse there adrian tell us more  about these sentences  well adrian the latest chapter in hong  kong's continuing  political drama has been taking place  here at west kowloon magistrates court  where  within the past hour the best known  young  political activist in hong kong joshua  wong has been sentenced  to 13 and a half months in jail he was  convicted  of inciting uh an unlawful assembly  as well as organizing an unlawful  assembly  agnes chao his close friend and  colleague in the  ongoing protest movement was convicted  uh given a 10-month sentence for  participating in  an illegal assembly and also a second  charge of  helping organize it the third person  ivan lam  received a seven month sentence now they  had faced  possibly a maximum sentence of up to  three  years but in spite of all that agnes  chao  is still in a pretty serious situation  because four months ago  she was arrested under the new national  security law  on suspicion of colluding with foreign  powers now she's been out on bail  or had been until she pleaded guilty a  week ago  and if she is convicted though on that  uh  if she is charged and convicted she  faces the prospect of you know  life in jail now barely a week goes by  adrian  without you know a young activist being  prosecuted in court  since early last june more than ten  000 people have been arrested a thousand  of them  are under the age of 18 and so far  there have been a total of just over 2  300 prosecutions it means  that the courts here in hong kong are  becoming clogged  in fact they're still processing people  who were  arrested during the occupy umbrella  movement  seven years ago so this is something  that's going to  tie up the courts for you know weeks  perhaps you know  years to come we know that uh joshua  wong  is no stranger of course to jail he has  done three  stints in prison before this will be his  his fourth  and of course he's been arrested no less  than 10  times it means of course that agnes chao  who turns 24 on thursday will be  spending  her birthday beginning a 10-month jail  sentence and remember  these three activists are all under the  age of 26 yet in many ways  they are now veterans of of the  pro-democracy movement  why adrian did the three plead guilty  well they decided to plead guilty uh  nine days ago after consultations  with their lawyers it hasn't been  explained exactly why  they decided to do that but of course it  meant that they avoided  a a lengthy trial perhaps they felt that  by pleading guilty they would get  a a shorter sentence they were certainly  facing the prospect of up to three years  in jail because that's the maximum  sentence  a magistrate's court here in hong kong  can give  but the fact that they have received you  know sentences in excess of six months  i think will probably bring a lot of  critical comments from  many governments around the world  especially the incoming  uh biden administration in the united  states because  the president-elect has said that he  believes that  china will be his foreign policy  priority and of course hong kong  in many ways is at the center of all of  that  al jazeera's adrian brown reporting live  there from hong kong adrian many thanks  indeed
 

